    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Word formation</span> is the process by which new words are created from existing words, usually by adding prefixes or suffixes (affixes), or by changing a word\'s grammatical category. <span class="learn-keyword">Nominalization</span> is one of the most common word-formation processes in English — converting verbs and adjectives into nouns.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Nominalization</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nominalization is the process of forming a noun from a word belonging to another word class — usually a verb or an adjective — typically by adding a suffix.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Verb to Noun Nominalization</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Suffix</th><th>Verb</th><th>Noun</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>-tion / -sion</td><td>educate, decide</td><td>education, decision</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-ment</td><td>develop, govern</td><td>development, government</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-ance / -ence</td><td>perform, differ</td><td>performance, difference</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-al</td><td>arrive, refuse</td><td>arrival, refusal</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-ing</td><td>build, read</td><td>building, reading</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-er / -or</td><td>teach, act</td><td>teacher, actor</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Adjective to Noun Nominalization</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Suffix</th><th>Adjective</th><th>Noun</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>-ness</td><td>kind, happy</td><td>kindness, happiness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-ity</td><td>active, equal</td><td>activity, equality</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-th</td><td>strong, wide</td><td>strength, width</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-cy</td><td>accurate, urgent</td><td>accuracy, urgency</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-ce</td><td>important, patient</td><td>importance, patience</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Affixation: Prefixes and Suffixes</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Prefix:</strong> An affix added BEFORE a root word (e.g., un-, dis-, re-, mis-, pre-, anti-)</li>\n  <li><strong>Suffix:</strong> An affix added AFTER a root word (e.g., -ful, -less, -able, -ize)</li>\n  <li>Prefixes usually change MEANING without changing word class (happy → unhappy: both adjectives)</li>\n  <li>Suffixes often change WORD CLASS (happy [adj] → happiness [noun])</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Words of the Week (Vocabulary)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Important vocabulary for this term: <strong>bombastic</strong> (using inflated language), <strong>dearth</strong> (scarcity), <strong>hiatus</strong> (a pause/gap), <strong>implacable</strong> (unable to be appeased), <strong>stingy</strong> (unwilling to give/spend), <strong>dour</strong> (stern, unfriendly), <strong>ebullient</strong> (cheerful and full of energy), <strong>ignominious</strong> (deserving disgrace), <strong>impinge</strong> (to have an impact on), <strong>tome</strong> (a large, heavy book).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Nominalization = converting verbs/adjectives into nouns via suffixes (-tion, -ment, -ness, -ity, etc.). Prefixes usually change meaning (un-, dis-, re-); suffixes usually change word class. WAEC frequently tests: \'The noun form of [verb/adjective] is...\'\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Word formation creates new words through affixation. Nominalization converts verbs (-tion, -ment, -ance, -al, -ing, -er) and adjectives (-ness, -ity, -th, -cy, -ce) into nouns. Prefixes (before root) often change meaning; suffixes (after root) often change word class.\n</div>\n  ',
