    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">noun</span> is a word that names a person, place, thing, idea, or quality. Nouns can stand alone or be expanded into <span class="learn-keyword">noun phrases</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">noun clauses</span>. Distinguishing these structures is essential for both grammar accuracy and sentence analysis in WAEC English.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Types of Nouns</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Common</td><td>General names for people, places, things</td><td>boy, city, table</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Proper</td><td>Specific names (capitalised)</td><td>Lagos, Aisha, Nigeria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Abstract</td><td>Names of ideas, qualities, states</td><td>honesty, freedom, joy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Concrete</td><td>Names of physical things</td><td>book, chair, mango</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Collective</td><td>Names of groups</td><td>flock, team, jury</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Countable</td><td>Can be counted; have plurals</td><td>book/books, child/children</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uncountable (Mass)</td><td>Cannot normally be counted; no plural</td><td>water, sugar, information</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Noun Phrases — Structure and Function</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A noun phrase (NP) is a group of words that functions like a noun in a sentence — it has a noun (the \'head\') and may include modifiers (determiners, adjectives) before it, and complements/modifiers after it.</p>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Structure:</strong> Determiner + Adjective(s) + Head Noun + (Modifier)<br>Example: <em>\'the tall, intelligent student from Kano\'</em> — \'student\' is the head noun.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Functions of Noun Phrases in a Sentence</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Subject:</strong> \'The young boy ran quickly.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Object (Direct):</strong> \'She bought a new dress.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Object of Preposition:</strong> \'He sat on the wooden chair.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Complement:</strong> \'He is a brilliant teacher.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Appositive:</strong> \'My friend, the doctor, called yesterday.\'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Noun Clauses</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A noun clause is a dependent clause that functions as a noun in a sentence — it has its own subject and verb but cannot stand alone. Noun clauses often begin with: <strong>that, what, who, whom, whether, why, how, when, where, if</strong>.</p>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>As Subject:</strong> \'What he said surprised everyone.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>As Object:</strong> \'I know that he is honest.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>As Complement:</strong> \'The truth is that she lied.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>As Object of Preposition:</strong> \'I am interested in what you propose.\'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Distinguishing Noun Phrases from Noun Clauses</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Noun Phrase</th><th>Noun Clause</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>No subject-verb structure of its own</td><td>Has its own subject and verb (finite verb)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\'the brilliant student\'</td><td>\'that the student is brilliant\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\'his arrival\'</td><td>\'when he arrived\'</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A NOUN CLAUSE has its own subject + verb (finite verb) and begins with words like that, what, whether, why, who. A NOUN PHRASE has no internal subject-verb structure — it\'s just a noun with modifiers. WAEC often asks you to identify the function of an underlined group of words (subject, object, complement).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nouns are classified as common/proper, abstract/concrete, collective, countable/uncountable. A noun phrase = noun + modifiers (no subject-verb). A noun clause = dependent clause with subject+verb functioning as a noun (subject, object, complement). Both can serve as subject, object, or complement in a sentence.\n</div>\n  ',
