    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">pronoun</span> is a word used in place of a noun to avoid repetition. English has several types of pronouns, each with a specific grammatical function. Correct pronoun usage — especially agreement and case — is one of the most heavily tested areas in WAEC Lexis and Structure.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Types of Pronouns</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Personal</td><td>I, you, he, she, it, we, they / me, him, her, us, them</td><td>Refer to specific people/things (subject or object form)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Possessive</td><td>mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs / my, your, his, her, our, their</td><td>Show ownership</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Demonstrative</td><td>this, that, these, those</td><td>Point to specific things</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Interrogative</td><td>who, whom, whose, which, what</td><td>Ask questions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reflexive</td><td>myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves, themselves</td><td>Refer back to the subject</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Relative</td><td>who, whom, whose, which, that</td><td>Introduce relative clauses; link to a noun</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reciprocal</td><td>each other, one another</td><td>Show mutual action between two or more</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Indefinite</td><td>someone, anything, everybody, nobody, few, several, all</td><td>Refer to non-specific people/things</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Personal Pronouns: Subject vs Object Case</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Subject (does the action)</th><th>Object (receives the action)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>I</td><td>me</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>he</td><td>him</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>she</td><td>her</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>we</td><td>us</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>they</td><td>them</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p">Common error: \'Between you and I\' — INCORRECT. Should be: \'Between you and me\' (object form, as object of preposition \'between\').</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Possessive: Determiner vs Pronoun</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Possessive determiners</strong> (my, your, his, her, our, their) come BEFORE a noun: \'This is <strong>my</strong> book.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Possessive pronouns</strong> (mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs) STAND ALONE, replacing a noun phrase: \'This book is <strong>mine</strong>.\'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Reflexive Pronouns</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Used when the subject and object of a verb refer to the same person/thing: \'She cut <strong>herself</strong>.\' Also used for emphasis: \'I will do it <strong>myself</strong>.\'</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Relative Pronouns</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>who</strong> — refers to people (subject): \'The man <strong>who</strong> called is my uncle.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>whom</strong> — refers to people (object): \'The man <strong>whom</strong> I met is my uncle.\'</li>\n  <li><strong>whose</strong> — possessive: \'The student <strong>whose</strong> book was lost...\'</li>\n  <li><strong>which</strong> — refers to things/animals: \'The car <strong>which</strong> I bought...\'</li>\n  <li><strong>that</strong> — refers to people or things (often in restrictive clauses): \'The book <strong>that</strong> I read...\'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WHO = subject (he did something); WHOM = object (something was done to him). Test: if you can replace with \'he/she\' use WHO; if you can replace with \'him/her\' use WHOM. Reciprocal pronouns (each other/one another) show mutual relationships between TWO OR MORE people. Indefinite pronouns like \'everyone\', \'nobody\', \'each\' take SINGULAR verbs.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Pronoun types: personal (subject/object), possessive (determiner/pronoun), demonstrative, interrogative, reflexive, relative, reciprocal, indefinite. Key distinctions: I/me (subject/object case); my/mine (determiner/pronoun); who/whom (subject/object); each other (two)/one another (more than two, traditionally). Indefinite pronouns (everyone, nobody) usually take singular verbs.\n</div>\n  ',
