    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  English nouns form their <span class="learn-keyword">plurals</span> in several ways — most follow regular patterns, but many common and important nouns have irregular plural forms that must be memorised. Compound nouns also follow special rules. This topic covers all major pluralization patterns tested in WAEC.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Regular Plural Formation</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Add -s (most nouns)</td><td>book→books, cat→cats, table→tables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Add -es (nouns ending in -s, -ss, -sh, -ch, -x, -z)</td><td>bus→buses, glass→glasses, brush→brushes, watch→watches, box→boxes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-y → -ies (consonant + y)</td><td>baby→babies, city→cities, lady→ladies</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-y → -ys (vowel + y)</td><td>boy→boys, day→days, key→keys</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-f/-fe → -ves (some nouns)</td><td>leaf→leaves, knife→knives, wife→wives, life→lives</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-f → -fs (other nouns)</td><td>roof→roofs, chief→chiefs, belief→beliefs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-o → -oes (some nouns)</td><td>potato→potatoes, tomato→tomatoes, hero→heroes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>-o → -os (other nouns, often loanwords)</td><td>photo→photos, piano→pianos, radio→radios</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Irregular Plurals</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Singular</th><th>Plural</th><th>Type of Change</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>man</td><td>men</td><td>Vowel change</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>woman</td><td>women</td><td>Vowel change</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>child</td><td>children</td><td>Different ending</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>tooth</td><td>teeth</td><td>Vowel change</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>foot</td><td>feet</td><td>Vowel change</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>mouse</td><td>mice</td><td>Vowel change</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>ox</td><td>oxen</td><td>Old English plural</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>sheep</td><td>sheep</td><td>No change (zero plural)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>deer</td><td>deer</td><td>No change (zero plural)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>fish</td><td>fish (or fishes)</td><td>No change (zero plural common)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>person</td><td>people / persons</td><td>Irregular</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Foreign-Origin Plurals (Latin/Greek)</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Singular</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>analysis</td><td>analyses</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>crisis</td><td>crises</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>criterion</td><td>criteria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>phenomenon</td><td>phenomena</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>datum</td><td>data</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>curriculum</td><td>curricula</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>cactus</td><td>cacti</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>stimulus</td><td>stimuli</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>thesis</td><td>theses</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Plurals of Compound Nouns</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Pluralize the main (head) word:</strong> mother-in-law → mothers-in-law; man-of-war → men-of-war; passer-by → passers-by</li>\n  <li><strong>If no clear head noun, pluralize the end:</strong> breakdowns, grown-ups</li>\n  <li><strong>Closed compounds:</strong> pluralize as normal: bedrooms, classrooms, footballs</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC frequently tests irregular plurals (man→men, child→children, tooth→teeth) and compound noun plurals (mother-in-law → mothers-in-law, NOT mother-in-laws). Watch for words with ZERO plural (sheep, deer, fish — same singular/plural). \'Data\' and \'criteria\' are technically plural (datum, criterion are singular), though \'data\' is often used as singular in modern usage.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Regular plurals: -s, -es (after s/ss/sh/ch/x/z), -ies (consonant+y), -ves (some -f/-fe words). Irregular: man→men, child→children, tooth→teeth, foot→feet, mouse→mice, sheep→sheep (no change). Foreign plurals: criterion→criteria, datum→data, analysis→analyses. Compound nouns: pluralize the HEAD noun (mothers-in-law, passers-by).\n</div>\n  ',
