    module: 'Lexis and Structure',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  An <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adverb</span> is a word that modifies a verb, adjective, another adverb, or an entire sentence — typically telling us HOW, WHEN, WHERE, HOW OFTEN, or TO WHAT DEGREE something happens. An <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adverbial phrase</span> is a group of words that together perform the function of an adverb.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Adverbs</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Question Answered</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Manner</td><td>How?</td><td>quickly, carefully, well, badly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Place</td><td>Where?</td><td>here, there, everywhere, abroad</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Time</td><td>When?</td><td>now, yesterday, soon, later</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Frequency</td><td>How often?</td><td>always, often, rarely, never</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Degree</td><td>To what extent?</td><td>very, extremely, quite, almost</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reason/Purpose</td><td>Why?</td><td>therefore, hence, consequently</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Formation of Adverbs</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Most adverbs of manner are formed by adding '-ly' to an adjective: quick → quickly, careful → carefully</li>\n  <li>Adjectives ending in '-y' change to '-ily': happy → happily, easy → easily</li>\n  <li>Adjectives ending in '-le' drop the 'e' and add '-y': simple → simply, gentle → gently</li>\n  <li><strong>Irregular adverbs:</strong> good → well; fast → fast (same form); hard → hard (same form, NOT 'hardly' which means 'barely')</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Adverbial Phrases</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An adverbial phrase is a group of words (without its own subject-verb structure) that functions as an adverb — modifying the verb by answering how, when, where, why, etc.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Adverbial Phrase</th><th>Type</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>'in the morning'</td><td>Time</td><td>'She exercises in the morning.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>'with great care'</td><td>Manner</td><td>'He repaired the watch with great care.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>'at the market'</td><td>Place</td><td>'They met at the market.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>'because of the rain'</td><td>Reason</td><td>'The match was cancelled because of the rain.'</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Position of Adverbs</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Adverbs of manner</strong> typically come AFTER the verb or object: 'She sings beautifully.'</li>\n  <li><strong>Adverbs of frequency</strong> typically come BEFORE the main verb but AFTER 'be': 'He always arrives early.' / 'He is always late.'</li>\n  <li><strong>Adverbs of time/place</strong> often come at the END of the sentence: 'We will meet tomorrow.'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Comparative and Superlative Adverbs</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Adverb</th><th>Comparative</th><th>Superlative</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>fast</td><td>faster</td><td>fastest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>carefully</td><td>more carefully</td><td>most carefully</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>well</td><td>better</td><td>best</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>badly</td><td>worse</td><td>worst</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Vocabulary: Words of Colour, Smell, and Taste</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Descriptive vocabulary related to senses: <strong>verdant</strong> (green with vegetation), <strong>pungent</strong> (a sharp, strong smell or taste), <strong>vapid</strong> (lacking taste/flavour, dull), <strong>serene</strong> (calm and peaceful — often used of colour/scenery), <strong>trite</strong> (overused, lacking originality).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> 'Hardly' does NOT mean 'in a hard manner' — it means 'barely/almost not' (e.g., 'He hardly studies' = he rarely studies). 'Hard' as an adverb means 'with effort' (e.g., 'He works hard'). Adverbs of frequency go BEFORE the main verb but AFTER 'be' verbs. Don't confuse adjectives (describe nouns) with adverbs (describe verbs/adjectives/adverbs).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs — types include manner, place, time, frequency, degree, reason. Most are formed with '-ly'. Adverbial phrases (groups of words without subject-verb) perform the same function. Position varies by type: manner (after verb), frequency (before main verb), time/place (often at sentence end). 'Hardly' ≠ 'hard' + 'ly' in meaning.\n</div>\n  ",
