    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Summary writing</span> is the skill of condensing a longer passage into a shorter version, retaining only the most important points in the writer\'s own words. It is a heavily examined skill in WAEC English, testing comprehension, selection, and concise expression.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Features of a Good Summary</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>A summary presents the MAIN IDEAS of a passage in a SHORTER form</li>\n  <li>It must be written in the SUMMARISER\'S OWN WORDS (not copied verbatim)</li>\n  <li>It must be OBJECTIVE — no personal opinions or additions</li>\n  <li>It must retain the LOGICAL ORDER/sequence of the original ideas</li>\n  <li>It must fit within the specified WORD LIMIT</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Steps for Writing a Summary</h3>\n<ol class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Read the passage carefully</strong> (more than once) to understand the overall meaning</li>\n  <li><strong>Identify the topic sentence</strong> of each paragraph — this usually contains the main idea</li>\n  <li><strong>Underline or note key points</strong> — eliminate examples, illustrations, repetitions</li>\n  <li><strong>Write the points in your own words</strong> — avoid copying phrases directly</li>\n  <li><strong>Combine points into coherent sentences</strong> using appropriate linking words</li>\n  <li><strong>Check the word count</strong> and ensure you have not exceeded the limit</li>\n  <li><strong>Proofread</strong> for grammar, spelling, and coherence</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Do\'s and Don\'ts of Summary Writing</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>DO</th><th>DON\'T</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Use your own words/paraphrase</td><td>Copy sentences directly from the passage</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Include only main ideas</td><td>Include examples, illustrations, or minor details</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Maintain the original sequence of ideas</td><td>Rearrange ideas in a confusing order</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Write in continuous prose (unless told otherwise)</td><td>Write in point form unless specifically instructed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Stay within the word limit</td><td>Add your own opinions, comments, or conclusions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Use linking words for coherence</td><td>Use direct/reported speech, abbreviations</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Summarising Arguments</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When summarising an ARGUMENTATIVE passage:</p>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Identify the WRITER\'S POSITION/THESIS (what is being argued for or against)</li>\n  <li>Identify the SUPPORTING POINTS/REASONS given for the argument</li>\n  <li>Note any COUNTER-ARGUMENTS the writer addresses</li>\n  <li>Present these points concisely without adding YOUR OWN opinion on the issue</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>serendipity</strong> (a pleasant surprise/happy accident), <strong>reprieve</strong> (a delay/cancellation of punishment), <strong>pariah</strong> (an outcast, despised person), <strong>maelstrom</strong> (a state of confused, violent movement), <strong>wistful</strong> (having sad/longing thoughts), <strong>xenophobia</strong> (fear/hatred of foreigners), <strong>upbraid</strong> (to criticise/scold severely), <strong>ephemeral</strong> (lasting a very short time), <strong>hierarchy</strong> (a ranked system/order), <strong>inept</strong> (lacking skill, incompetent), <strong>rectitude</strong> (moral correctness/honesty), <strong>abdicate</strong> (to give up power/responsibility formally), <strong>apathetic</strong> (showing no interest/concern).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Summary writing rules: (1) NEVER copy verbatim — paraphrase; (2) Drop examples/illustrations — keep only main points; (3) Stay within word limit (going over loses marks); (4) Write in continuous prose unless instructed otherwise; (5) Don\'t add your own opinion. When the question asks for a specific number of points, count carefully and present exactly that number.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> A good summary: shorter than original, in your own words, objective (no added opinions), maintains logical order, within word limit. Steps: read carefully, identify topic sentences, note key points (drop examples), paraphrase, combine coherently, check word count, proofread. For argumentative passages: identify thesis, supporting points, counter-arguments — without adding your own opinion.\n</div>\n  ',
