    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Expository essays</span> explain or inform the reader about a topic using facts, examples, and analysis. <span class="learn-keyword">Argumentative essays</span> take a position on a debatable issue and persuade the reader using evidence and reasoning. Both follow a clear structural framework.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Structure of an Expository Essay</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Introduction</td><td>States the topic and gives a brief overview/background; may state the thesis (what aspects will be discussed)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Body paragraphs</td><td>Each paragraph explores ONE aspect/cause/effect/solution with facts, examples, and explanation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Conclusion</td><td>Summarises the main points; may offer a final reflection or call to action</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example topic:</strong> \'Controlling HIV/AIDS in Nigeria\' — body paragraphs might cover: causes of spread, prevention methods, government interventions, role of education, challenges faced.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Structure of an Argumentative Essay</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> States the issue and the writer\'s CLEAR POSITION (for/against)</li>\n  <li><strong>Body paragraphs:</strong> Each paragraph presents ONE POINT supporting the writer\'s position, with evidence and reasoning. May also ACKNOWLEDGE and REBUT opposing views</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Restates the position and summarises the strongest arguments; may end with a recommendation</li>\n</ul>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example topics:</strong> \'Should female circumcision be abolished?\' / \'Military rule is better than civilian rule.\' — writer must take a clear stance and support it with logical reasoning.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Key Differences: Expository vs Argumentative</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Expository</th><th>Argumentative</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Purpose: to INFORM/explain</td><td>Purpose: to PERSUADE</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Presents multiple perspectives objectively</td><td>Takes a CLEAR POSITION/SIDE</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uses facts and explanations</td><td>Uses facts, evidence, AND persuasive reasoning</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>May not require a \'for/against\' stance</td><td>Must address counter-arguments</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Linking Words for Essays</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Linking Words</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Adding points</td><td>furthermore, moreover, in addition, also</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Contrast</td><td>however, on the other hand, nevertheless, although</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cause/effect</td><td>therefore, consequently, as a result, thus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>for instance, for example, such as</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Conclusion</td><td>in conclusion, to sum up, in summary, finally</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>connive</strong> (to secretly plan something illegal/harmful), <strong>daunting</strong> (intimidating, discouraging), <strong>promulgate</strong> (to formally proclaim/put into effect), <strong>rancour</strong> (deep bitterness/resentment), <strong>serene</strong> (calm, peaceful), <strong>clandestine</strong> (secret, hidden), <strong>prescient</strong> (having foreknowledge of events), <strong>servile</strong> (excessively obedient/submissive), <strong>torpid</strong> (sluggish, lacking energy), <strong>trite</strong> (overused, lacking originality), <strong>pliable</strong> (easily bent/influenced), <strong>pittance</strong> (a very small amount of money).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Expository = explain/inform (multiple aspects, balanced). Argumentative = persuade (clear position FOR or AGAINST + addresses counter-arguments). Both need: clear introduction with topic statement, body paragraphs each developing ONE idea, and a conclusion that summarises. WAEC penalises essays without a clear introduction/conclusion or that wander off-topic.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Expository essay: informs/explains, multiple perspectives, structured around aspects/causes/effects. Argumentative essay: persuades, takes a CLEAR position, addresses counter-arguments. Both have introduction-body-conclusion structure. Use linking words appropriately (furthermore, however, therefore, for instance, in conclusion) to connect ideas coherently.\n</div>\n  ',
