    contentHTML:
      "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Punctuation marks</span> are symbols used in writing to separate sentences, clarify meaning, and indicate pauses or relationships between ideas. Correct punctuation is essential for clear written communication and is a frequently tested area in WAEC Lexis and Structure.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Possessive Apostrophe</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Singular noun: add 's</td><td>the boy's bag, James's car</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plural noun ending in -s: add only '</td><td>the boys' bag, the students' results</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plural noun NOT ending in -s: add 's</td><td>the children's toys, the men's room</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Joint possession (two owners, one item)</td><td>John and Mary's house (they share one house)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Separate possession (two owners, separate items)</td><td>John's and Mary's houses (each has their own)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Contractions (apostrophe shows omitted letters)</td><td>don't (do not), it's (it is), can't (cannot)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Important distinction:</strong> 'its' (possessive, no apostrophe) vs 'it's' (contraction of 'it is'/'it has'). 'The dog wagged its tail' (possessive) vs 'It's raining' (it is raining).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Question Mark and Exclamation Mark</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Question mark (?):</strong> Used at the end of a DIRECT question. 'Where are you going?' (NOT used for indirect/reported questions: 'He asked where I was going.')</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclamation mark (!):</strong> Used to express strong emotion, surprise, or emphasis. 'What a beautiful sunset!' / 'Stop right there!'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Colon (:)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Introduces a LIST: 'Bring the following: a pen, a notebook, and a calculator.'</li>\n  <li>Introduces an EXPLANATION or ELABORATION: 'There was one reason for his success: hard work.'</li>\n  <li>Introduces a QUOTATION (formal): 'The teacher said: \"Practice makes perfect.\"'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The Semi-colon (;)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Joins two CLOSELY RELATED independent clauses without a conjunction: 'She studied hard; she passed the exam.'</li>\n  <li>Separates items in a COMPLEX LIST (especially when items themselves contain commas): 'We visited Lagos, Nigeria; Accra, Ghana; and Dakar, Senegal.'</li>\n  <li>Often used before connecting words like 'however', 'therefore', 'moreover': 'He was tired; however, he continued working.'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. The Hyphen (-)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Joins words to form COMPOUND adjectives before a noun: 'a well-known author', 'a five-year-old child'</li>\n  <li>Joins NUMBERS written as words: 'twenty-one', 'forty-five'</li>\n  <li>Used with certain PREFIXES: 'ex-president', 'self-control', 'co-operate'</li>\n  <li>Avoids ambiguity: 're-cover' (cover again) vs 'recover' (get better)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>morass</strong> (a complicated/messy situation; also a marsh), <strong>abject</strong> (extremely bad/miserable), <strong>blandish</strong> (to coax with flattery), <strong>duplicity</strong> (deceitfulness), <strong>ennui</strong> (boredom/listlessness), <strong>maudlin</strong> (self-pityingly sentimental), <strong>abscond</strong> (to leave secretly to escape), <strong>alias</strong> (a false/assumed name), <strong>dither</strong> (to hesitate/be indecisive), <strong>emaciated</strong> (abnormally thin from illness/hunger).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Plural noun ending in -s gets ONLY an apostrophe (boys'); singular nouns and plurals NOT ending in -s get 's (boy's, children's). 'Its' = possessive; 'It's' = it is/it has. Colon introduces lists/explanations; semicolon joins related independent clauses. Question marks ONLY for direct questions, never reported/indirect questions.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Apostrophe: 's for singular/irregular plural, ' alone for regular plural, contractions for omitted letters. Question mark: direct questions only. Exclamation mark: strong emotion. Colon: introduces lists/explanations. Semi-colon: joins related independent clauses or separates complex list items. Hyphen: compound adjectives, written numbers, certain prefixes, avoiding ambiguity.\n</div>\n  ",
