    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">formal letter</span> is a written communication addressed to someone in an official, professional, or business capacity. The <span class="learn-keyword">letter of complaint</span> is one of the most commonly examined formal letter types in WAEC — it requires a clear, polite, but firm tone.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Layout of a Formal Letter</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Position/Content</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Writer\'s address</td><td>Top right corner (no name)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Date</td><td>Below the writer\'s address</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Recipient\'s address</td><td>Left side, below the date (includes name/title if known)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Salutation</td><td>\'Dear Sir/Madam,\' or \'Dear Mr./Mrs. [Surname],\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Subject heading (optional but recommended)</td><td>Underlined, summarising the letter\'s purpose</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Body</td><td>Introduction, main content (in paragraphs), conclusion/request</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Complimentary close</td><td>\'Yours faithfully,\' (if salutation is Sir/Madam) or \'Yours sincerely,\' (if salutation uses a name)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Signature</td><td>Full name (and sometimes designation) below the close</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Structure of a Letter of Complaint</h3>\n<ol class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Paragraph 1:</strong> State the purpose of the letter and what is being complained about</li>\n  <li><strong>Paragraph 2:</strong> Give details of the problem (what happened, when, where, who was involved)</li>\n  <li><strong>Paragraph 3:</strong> Explain the effect/inconvenience caused by the problem</li>\n  <li><strong>Paragraph 4:</strong> State the desired action/resolution clearly (refund, replacement, apology, repair)</li>\n  <li><strong>Closing:</strong> Express hope for prompt action; thank the recipient</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Tone and Language</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Maintain a POLITE but FIRM tone — avoid being rude or aggressive</li>\n  <li>Be SPECIFIC — include dates, reference numbers, names where relevant</li>\n  <li>Use FORMAL register — avoid slang, contractions (don\'t write \'I didn\'t\', write \'I did not\')</li>\n  <li>State clearly what RESOLUTION you expect</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Example Salutation/Closing Pairs</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Salutation</th><th>Complimentary Close</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Dear Sir/Madam,</td><td>Yours faithfully,</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dear Mr. Adamu,</td><td>Yours sincerely,</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dear Manager,</td><td>Yours faithfully,</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Vocabulary Development: Press and Journalism Words</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>headline</strong> (the title of a news article), <strong>byline</strong> (the writer\'s name on an article), <strong>editorial</strong> (an article expressing the publisher\'s opinion), <strong>scoop</strong> (an exclusive news story), <strong>tabloid</strong> (a newspaper with sensational, brief stories), <strong>columnist</strong> (a writer of a regular opinion piece), <strong>circulation</strong> (the number of copies distributed), <strong>correspondent</strong> (a reporter, often abroad/specialised), <strong>press release</strong> (an official statement issued to media), <strong>libel</strong> (a published false statement damaging reputation).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Match the closing to the salutation: \'Dear Sir/Madam\' → \'Yours faithfully\'; \'Dear Mr./Mrs. [Name]\' → \'Yours sincerely\'. Never use contractions in formal letters (write \'do not\', not \'don\'t\'). Letter of complaint structure: state problem → give details → explain effect → state desired resolution. Always include the writer\'s address but NOT the writer\'s name above it.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Formal letter layout: writer\'s address (top right, no name), date, recipient\'s address (left), salutation, subject heading, body (intro-details-effect-resolution), complimentary close matched to salutation, signature. Letter of complaint: polite but firm tone, specific details (dates/names), formal register (no contractions/slang), clear statement of desired resolution.\n</div>\n  ',
