    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  An <span class="learn-keyword">informal letter</span> is a personal letter written to family or friends, using a relaxed, conversational tone. Unlike formal letters, informal letters allow for warmth, humour, and personal expression, while still following a recognisable structure.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Layout of an Informal Letter</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Writer\'s address</td><td>Top right corner (simpler than formal — may omit postal code in some styles)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Date</td><td>Below the address</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Salutation</td><td>\'Dear [First Name],\' e.g., \'Dear Chioma,\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Opening</td><td>A friendly opening line — asking about wellbeing, referencing previous contact</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Body</td><td>Main content in a warm, conversational tone — news, stories, feelings</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Closing</td><td>A friendly closing remark (looking forward to a reply, sending regards)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Complimentary close</td><td>\'Yours affectionately,\' \'Love,\' \'Your friend,\' \'Best wishes,\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Signature</td><td>First name only (no surname needed)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Differences Between Formal and Informal Letters</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Formal Letter</th><th>Informal Letter</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Salutation</td><td>Dear Sir/Madam, Dear Mr. [Surname],</td><td>Dear [First Name],</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tone</td><td>Polite, professional, distant</td><td>Warm, friendly, personal</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Language</td><td>No contractions, formal vocabulary</td><td>Contractions allowed, colloquial expressions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Complimentary close</td><td>Yours faithfully/sincerely,</td><td>Love, Best wishes, Your friend,</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Signature</td><td>Full name</td><td>First name only</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Content</td><td>Single, focused purpose</td><td>Multiple topics, personal news</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Sample Content for \'Letter to a Friend in Another Town\'</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Opening pleasantries (How are you? How is your family?)</li>\n  <li>News about your own life (school, family, recent events)</li>\n  <li>Questions about the friend\'s life/situation</li>\n  <li>An invitation, request, or shared plan</li>\n  <li>Closing with affection and anticipation of a reply</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>cherish</strong> (to hold dear, treasure), <strong>capricious</strong> (unpredictable, changing mood/behaviour suddenly), <strong>eschew</strong> (to deliberately avoid), <strong>perusal</strong> (a careful reading/examination), <strong>renunciation</strong> (formal rejection/giving up of something), <strong>spurious</strong> (false, not genuine), <strong>tenable</strong> (able to be defended/maintained, as an argument), <strong>turgid</strong> (overly complex/pompous in language; also swollen), <strong>antediluvian</strong> (extremely old-fashioned/outdated).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Informal letters use FIRST NAME ONLY in salutation and signature. Contractions ARE allowed (unlike formal letters). Closing phrases like \'Love,\' \'Best wishes,\' or \'Your friend,\' replace \'Yours faithfully/sincerely.\' Content can cover MULTIPLE topics (unlike formal letters, which focus on one issue). WAEC examiners check for appropriate WARMTH and PERSONAL tone in informal letters.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Informal letter layout: address, date, \'Dear [First Name],\' friendly opening, conversational body (multiple topics allowed), warm closing, affectionate complimentary close (Love/Best wishes), first name signature. Differs from formal letters in: tone (warm vs professional), language (contractions allowed), salutation/close style, and content focus (personal vs single purpose).\n</div>\n  ',
