    topic: 'Sequence of Tenses',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ENG-13',
    module: 'Lexis and Structure',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The <span class="learn-keyword">sequence of tenses</span> refers to the grammatical rules governing how the tense of a verb in one clause relates to (and is often determined by) the tense of the verb in another clause within the same sentence — especially in REPORTED SPEECH and complex sentences.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Basic Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When the main (reporting) clause is in the PAST TENSE, the verb in the subordinate clause usually also shifts to a PAST form — this is called \'backshift\'.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Direct/Original</th><th>Reported (after past reporting verb)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Present Simple: \'I am happy.\'</td><td>Past Simple: He said he was happy.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Present Continuous: \'I am eating.\'</td><td>Past Continuous: He said he was eating.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Present Perfect: \'I have finished.\'</td><td>Past Perfect: He said he had finished.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Past Simple: \'I went home.\'</td><td>Past Perfect: He said he had gone home.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Will: \'I will come.\'</td><td>Would: He said he would come.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Can: \'I can swim.\'</td><td>Could: He said he could swim.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Exceptions to Backshift</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Universal/general truths:</strong> No backshift needed — \'The teacher said the earth revolves around the sun\' (NOT \'revolved\', because this is always true)</li>\n  <li><strong>If the reporting verb is in the PRESENT tense:</strong> No backshift — \'He says he IS tired\' (not \'was\')</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Sequence of Tenses in Complex Sentences (Time Clauses)</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>In clauses introduced by \'when\', \'before\', \'after\', \'since\', \'as soon as\' — the SAME general time frame is usually maintained: \'When she ARRIVED, we WERE waiting.\' (both past)</li>\n  <li>\'Since\' + Past Simple is used with Present Perfect in the main clause: \'I have lived here SINCE I WAS born.\'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Conditional Sentences and Tense Sequence</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>If-clause</th><th>Main clause</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Zero (general truth)</td><td>Present Simple</td><td>Present Simple</td><td>If you heat water, it boils.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>First (real future)</td><td>Present Simple</td><td>Will + base verb</td><td>If it rains, I will stay home.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Second (unreal present)</td><td>Past Simple</td><td>Would + base verb</td><td>If I were rich, I would travel.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Third (unreal past)</td><td>Past Perfect</td><td>Would have + past participle</td><td>If I had studied, I would have passed.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>propriety</strong> (correctness of behaviour/morals), <strong>exigent</strong> (urgent, requiring immediate action), <strong>forestall</strong> (to prevent by acting first), <strong>tranquil</strong> (calm, peaceful), <strong>heterogeneous</strong> (diverse, made of different elements), <strong>unctuous</strong> (excessively flattering/insincere), <strong>canvass</strong> (to solicit votes/opinions), <strong>carouse</strong> (to drink and enjoy oneself noisily), <strong>nefarious</strong> (wicked, criminal), <strong>novice</strong> (a beginner, inexperienced person).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Backshift rule: PAST reporting verb → subordinate clause verb shifts BACK one tense (present→past, past→past perfect). NO backshift for universal truths or when reporting verb is present tense. Conditionals: Zero/First use present forms; Second uses past simple + would; Third uses past perfect + would have + past participle.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Sequence of tenses: when reporting verb is PAST, subordinate clause backshifts (present→past, past→past perfect, will→would, can→could). Exceptions: universal truths (no backshift), present reporting verb (no backshift). Time clauses maintain consistent time frame. Conditionals: Zero (present+present), First (present+will), Second (past+would), Third (past perfect+would have+participle).\n</div>\n  ',
