    contentHTML:
      "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">phrasal verb</span> is a verb combined with one or more particles (prepositions and/or adverbs) to create a meaning that is often different from the individual words. Phrasal verbs are extremely common in everyday English and frequently tested in WAEC.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Common Phrasal Verbs and Their Meanings</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Phrasal Verb</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>look up to</td><td>admire/respect</td><td>'She looks up to her mother.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>put up with</td><td>tolerate</td><td>'I can't put up with his rudeness.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>look forward to</td><td>anticipate eagerly</td><td>'We look forward to seeing you.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>give up</td><td>stop trying / surrender</td><td>'Don't give up on your dreams.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>come across</td><td>find by chance</td><td>'I came across an old photo.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>break down</td><td>stop functioning / become emotional</td><td>'The car broke down on the highway.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>carry out</td><td>perform/execute (a task)</td><td>'They carried out the experiment.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>turn down</td><td>reject / lower volume</td><td>'She turned down the job offer.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>run out of</td><td>have no more of something</td><td>'We ran out of fuel.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>look down on</td><td>regard as inferior</td><td>'He looks down on people less educated than him.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>get away with</td><td>escape punishment for wrongdoing</td><td>'He won't get away with this.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>put off</td><td>postpone</td><td>'They put off the meeting until Monday.'</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Phrasal Verbs</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Two-word (single particle):</strong> give up, break down, carry out</li>\n  <li><strong>Three-word (two particles):</strong> look forward to, put up with, look down on, get away with</li>\n  <li><strong>Transitive:</strong> takes a direct object — 'turn down the offer'</li>\n  <li><strong>Intransitive:</strong> no direct object — 'the car broke down'</li>\n  <li><strong>Separable:</strong> object can go between verb and particle — 'turn the offer down' / 'turn down the offer'</li>\n  <li><strong>Inseparable:</strong> object must come AFTER the full phrasal verb — 'look after the children' (NOT 'look the children after')</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Vocabulary Development: Words Associated with Building and Construction</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>culvert</strong> (a drain/channel allowing water to pass under a road), <strong>foundation</strong> (the base structure of a building), <strong>scaffold</strong> (temporary structure for workers during construction), <strong>masonry</strong> (stonework/brickwork), <strong>girder</strong> (a large beam supporting a structure), <strong>cantilever</strong> (a beam fixed at one end, projecting), <strong>parapet</strong> (a low protective wall at the edge of a roof/balcony), <strong>facade</strong> (the front face of a building).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>asylum</strong> (protection given to refugees; also a mental institution), <strong>consensus</strong> (general agreement among a group), <strong>hospice</strong> (a care facility for the terminally ill), <strong>wishy-washy</strong> (lacking conviction/strength of character), <strong>unsightly</strong> (unpleasant to look at).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Phrasal verbs CANNOT usually be guessed from their individual words — they must be MEMORISED as units. 'Put up with' = tolerate (NOT about placing something up). Separable phrasal verbs allow the object BETWEEN verb and particle when the object is a PRONOUN: 'turn it down' (NOT 'turn down it'). Inseparable ones never split: 'look after them' (NOT 'look them after').\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Phrasal verbs = verb + particle(s) with idiomatic meaning. Common ones: look up to (admire), put up with (tolerate), give up (stop), come across (find by chance), break down (stop functioning), carry out (perform), turn down (reject), run out of (deplete), get away with (escape punishment), put off (postpone). Types: two-word/three-word, transitive/intransitive, separable/inseparable.\n</div>\n  ",
