    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Voice</span> in grammar refers to whether the subject of a sentence PERFORMS the action (active voice) or RECEIVES the action (passive voice). Understanding how to transform sentences between active and passive voice is a core WAEC structure skill.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Active Voice</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">In active voice, the SUBJECT performs the action expressed by the verb.</p>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Structure:</strong> Subject + Verb + Object<br>Example: \'The chef <strong>cooked</strong> the meal.\' (Chef = doer of the action)</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Passive Voice</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">In passive voice, the SUBJECT receives the action — the original object becomes the new subject.</p>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Structure:</strong> Object (now subject) + BE + Past Participle + (by + original subject)<br>Example: \'The meal <strong>was cooked</strong> by the chef.\' (Meal = receiver of the action)</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Forming the Passive Voice by Tense</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tense</th><th>Active</th><th>Passive</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Present Simple</td><td>writes</td><td>is/are written</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Present Continuous</td><td>is writing</td><td>is being written</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Present Perfect</td><td>has written</td><td>has been written</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Past Simple</td><td>wrote</td><td>was/were written</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Past Continuous</td><td>was writing</td><td>was being written</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Past Perfect</td><td>had written</td><td>had been written</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Future Simple</td><td>will write</td><td>will be written</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Modal (can/must/should)</td><td>can write</td><td>can be written</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. When to Use Passive Voice</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>When the DOER of the action is unknown, unimportant, or obvious: \'The window was broken.\' (we don\'t know/care who)</li>\n  <li>When the ACTION or RESULT is more important than who did it (common in scientific/formal writing): \'The experiment was conducted carefully.\'</li>\n  <li>To avoid blame or be diplomatic: \'Mistakes were made.\' (avoids naming who made them)</li>\n  <li>When the OBJECT/RECEIVER is the focus of the sentence/discussion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Sentences That Cannot Be Made Passive</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>INTRANSITIVE verbs (no direct object) cannot be passivized: \'He arrived late\' → CANNOT become passive (no object to become the new subject)</li>\n  <li>Linking verbs (be, seem, become) generally cannot be passivized: \'She is a doctor\' → no passive form</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Vocabulary Development: Words Associated with Technology</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>email</strong>, <strong>satellite</strong>, <strong>virus</strong> (malicious software), <strong>password</strong>, <strong>bandwidth</strong>, <strong>firewall</strong>, <strong>download/upload</strong>, <strong>server</strong>, <strong>encryption</strong>, <strong>algorithm</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">7. Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>platitude</strong> (an unoriginal, overused remark), <strong>feisty</strong> (spirited, full of energy/determination), <strong>oust</strong> (to remove forcibly from a position), <strong>disarmament</strong> (the reduction/withdrawal of military weapons), <strong>vestige</strong> (a remaining trace of something that once existed), <strong>exude</strong> (to display a quality strongly/visibly), <strong>tantalizing</strong> (causing desire by being just out of reach), <strong>unbridled</strong> (uncontrolled, unrestrained).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Formula: Passive = BE (correct tense/form) + PAST PARTICIPLE + (by + agent, optional). The ORIGINAL OBJECT becomes the NEW SUBJECT. Intransitive verbs (arrive, happen, sleep) CANNOT be made passive — they have no object. The \'by + agent\' phrase is often OMITTED when the doer is unknown/unimportant/obvious.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Active voice: subject performs action (S+V+O). Passive voice: subject receives action (O→new S + BE + past participle + by + original S). Passive used when doer is unknown/unimportant, action matters more than doer, or for diplomacy. Formula varies by tense (is written, was written, has been written, will be written). Intransitive verbs and linking verbs generally cannot be passivized.\n</div>\n  ',
