    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Creative writing</span> (short stories, plays, poems) allows imaginative expression following genre conventions. <span class="learn-keyword">Article writing</span> is a distinct journalistic form intended for publication, with its own structural and stylistic features that differ from letters and essays.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Elements of a Short Story</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Plot</td><td>The sequence of events: exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Characters</td><td>The people/beings in the story (protagonist, antagonist, supporting characters)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>Time and place where the story occurs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Point of view</td><td>First person (I/we), third person limited, or third person omniscient</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Theme</td><td>The central message/idea explored in the story</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Conflict</td><td>The central problem/tension driving the plot (person vs person, vs self, vs society, vs nature)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Features of an Article</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Title/Headline:</strong> Catchy, attention-grabbing, summarising the topic</li>\n  <li><strong>By-line:</strong> Author\'s name (optional in exam context)</li>\n  <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> Hooks the reader; introduces the topic/issue</li>\n  <li><strong>Body:</strong> Develops the topic with facts, opinions, examples (organised in paragraphs)</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Summarises or gives a final thought/recommendation</li>\n  <li><strong>Intended for PUBLICATION</strong> — written for a general/specific readership, not a single recipient</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Articles vs Letters: Key Differences</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Article</th><th>Letter</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Audience</td><td>General public/readership</td><td>A specific individual/recipient</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Layout</td><td>Title, no address/salutation needed</td><td>Includes addresses, salutation, complimentary close</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tone</td><td>Can be informative, persuasive, or analytical</td><td>Personal (informal) or formal-official (formal)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>To inform, persuade, or entertain a wide readership</td><td>To communicate with one specific person/organisation</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Poetic Devices for Creative Writing</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Simile:</strong> Comparison using \'like\' or \'as\' — \'Brave as a lion\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Metaphor:</strong> Direct comparison without \'like/as\' — \'Life is a journey\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Personification:</strong> Giving human qualities to non-human things — \'The wind whispered\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Alliteration:</strong> Repetition of initial consonant sounds — \'Sally sells seashells\'</li>\n  <li><strong>Rhyme scheme:</strong> Pattern of rhyming line endings — labelled ABAB, AABB, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Vocabulary Development: Words Associated with Environment</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>deforestation</strong>, <strong>biodegradable</strong>, <strong>ecosystem</strong>, <strong>conservation</strong>, <strong>pollution</strong>, <strong>sustainability</strong>, <strong>greenhouse effect</strong>, <strong>renewable energy</strong>, <strong>erosion</strong>, <strong>desertification</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>paucity</strong> (a scarcity/insufficient amount), <strong>ostracism</strong> (exclusion from a group/society), <strong>acclaim</strong> (praise/enthusiastic approval), <strong>acerbic</strong> (sharp/bitter in tone), <strong>bereft</strong> (deprived of/lacking something, especially through loss), <strong>nomadic</strong> (moving from place to place, not settled), <strong>beguile</strong> (to charm/deceive), <strong>munificence</strong> (great generosity), <strong>phlegmatic</strong> (calm, unemotional temperament).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Articles need a CATCHY TITLE but NO address/salutation (unlike letters). Short stories need: plot (exposition→climax→resolution), characters, setting, theme, conflict, point of view. Key article-letter distinction: articles target a GENERAL readership; letters target ONE specific recipient. Use poetic devices (simile, metaphor, personification, alliteration) to enrich creative writing.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Short story elements: plot, characters, setting, point of view, theme, conflict. Article features: title, no salutation, intro-body-conclusion for general readership (unlike letters, addressed to ONE person). Poetic devices: simile (like/as), metaphor (direct comparison), personification (human qualities to non-human), alliteration (repeated initial sounds), rhyme scheme.\n</div>\n  ',
