    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Conditional clauses</span> express conditions and their consequences — what happens (or would happen) if a certain situation occurs. They are classified by the LIKELIHOOD of the condition being fulfilled: likely, unlikely, or impossible.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Conditional Clauses by Likelihood</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Likelihood</th><th>Structure</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>LIKELY (real future possibility)</td><td>If + Present Simple, + will + base verb</td><td>\'If I study hard, I will pass the exam.\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>UNLIKELY (hypothetical present/future)</td><td>If + Past Simple, + would + base verb</td><td>\'If I won the lottery, I would buy a house.\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>IMPOSSIBLE (unreal past — cannot be changed)</td><td>If + Past Perfect, + would have + past participle</td><td>\'If I had studied harder, I would have passed.\' (but I didn\'t, so I failed — unchangeable past)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Complex Sentences</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A complex sentence contains ONE independent clause and AT LEAST ONE dependent (subordinate) clause, joined by a SUBORDINATING CONJUNCTION.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Subordinating Conjunctions</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Time</td><td>when, while, before, after, since, as soon as, until</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reason/Cause</td><td>because, since, as</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Condition</td><td>if, unless, provided that, in case</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Contrast/Concession</td><td>although, though, even though, whereas</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>so that, in order that</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Constructing Complex Sentences</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>If the dependent clause comes FIRST, use a COMMA after it: \'Although it rained, the match continued.\'</li>\n  <li>If the dependent clause comes SECOND (after the independent clause), NO comma is typically needed: \'The match continued although it rained.\'</li>\n  <li>A dependent clause CANNOT stand alone as a complete sentence — it needs the independent clause to be grammatically complete</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Vocabulary Development: Words Associated with Publishing and Printing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>manuscript</strong> (an author\'s original text before publication), <strong>proofread</strong> (to check for errors before final printing), <strong>typeset</strong> (to arrange text for printing), <strong>publisher</strong> (the company that produces and distributes a book), <strong>royalty</strong> (payment to an author based on sales), <strong>ISBN</strong> (a unique book identification number), <strong>edition</strong> (a version of a published work), <strong>print run</strong> (the number of copies printed at once).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>acronym</strong> (a word formed from initial letters), <strong>troupe</strong> (a group of performers), <strong>ardour</strong> (intense passion/enthusiasm), <strong>behemoth</strong> (something huge/enormous), <strong>calumny</strong> (a false, damaging statement made to harm someone\'s reputation), <strong>indigent</strong> (extremely poor), <strong>dissemble</strong> (to conceal one\'s true feelings/intentions).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Likely = Present Simple + will (real possibility). Unlikely = Past Simple + would (hypothetical, but possible). Impossible = Past Perfect + would have + past participle (unreal past, cannot be changed). Complex sentences need a SUBORDINATING conjunction (because, although, when, if) joining an independent and a dependent clause. Comma needed when dependent clause comes FIRST.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Conditional clauses by likelihood: Likely (Present+will), Unlikely (Past+would), Impossible (Past Perfect+would have+participle). Complex sentences: independent clause + dependent clause joined by subordinating conjunction (time, reason, condition, contrast, purpose categories). Comma rule: dependent clause FIRST needs comma; dependent clause SECOND usually doesn\'t.\n</div>\n  ',
