    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">speech</span> is a formal address delivered orally to an audience, designed to inform, persuade, entertain, or mark a special occasion. WAEC tests specific TYPES of speeches: welcome address, farewell address, and prize-giving day address, each with distinct conventions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. General Structure of a Speech</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Salutation/Greeting:</strong> Addresses the audience appropriately (e.g., \'Good morning, distinguished guests, fellow students...\')</li>\n  <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> States the purpose/occasion of the speech</li>\n  <li><strong>Body:</strong> Develops the main message in a logical sequence</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Summarises key points; ends memorably (often with thanks or a call to action)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Welcome Address</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Purpose: To formally welcome guests/visitors to an event</li>\n  <li>Content: Greeting, acknowledgement of important guests, brief background of the event/occasion, expression of pleasure at their presence</li>\n  <li>Tone: Warm, welcoming, appreciative</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Farewell Address</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Purpose: To bid goodbye to someone leaving (graduating, retiring, relocating)</li>\n  <li>Content: Appreciation for the person\'s contributions, fond memories/highlights, well-wishes for the future</li>\n  <li>Tone: Reflective, appreciative, warm, sometimes nostalgic</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Prize-Giving Day Address</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Purpose: To celebrate and recognise achievement at an awards ceremony</li>\n  <li>Content: Congratulations to award recipients, acknowledgement of effort and hard work, encouragement for continued excellence, possibly remarks on the institution\'s achievements</li>\n  <li>Tone: Celebratory, congratulatory, motivational</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Differences Between a Speech and an Article</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Speech</th><th>Article</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Delivery</td><td>Spoken aloud to a live audience</td><td>Written for readers (print/online)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Opening</td><td>Direct address/greeting to the audience</td><td>Catchy title/headline</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Language</td><td>More rhetorical devices, direct address (\'you\', \'we\')</td><td>More analytical/descriptive, can use third person throughout</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Engagement</td><td>Uses pauses, repetition, vocal emphasis cues</td><td>Uses formatting, subheadings, paragraphing</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>complicit</strong> (involved with others in an illegal/wrongful act), <strong>impecunious</strong> (having little/no money), <strong>encumber</strong> (to weigh down/hinder), <strong>mollify</strong> (to calm/soothe someone\'s anger), <strong>frivolous</strong> (not serious/silly, especially when seriousness is needed), <strong>disrepute</strong> (a state of having a bad reputation), <strong>benevolent</strong> (kind and generous).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Speech writing must include DIRECT ADDRESS to the audience (\'Ladies and gentlemen...\', \'Fellow students...\'). Welcome address = greeting and appreciation. Farewell = thanks + good wishes. Prize-giving = congratulations + encouragement. Unlike an article, a speech is meant to be SPOKEN, so use rhetorical devices (repetition, direct questions to audience) and a conversational, engaging tone.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Speech structure: salutation, introduction (purpose/occasion), body (main message), conclusion (summary/memorable ending). Types: Welcome address (greet/appreciate), Farewell address (thank/reflect/wish well), Prize-giving address (congratulate/encourage). Differs from article: spoken not written, direct audience address, rhetorical devices, conversational engagement vs analytical reading.\n</div>\n  ',
