    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  An <span class="learn-keyword">article for publication</span> is written specifically for a newspaper, magazine, or online platform, intended to inform, persuade, or engage a general readership on a topic of interest. WAEC expects candidates to master the specific features and writing techniques of this genre.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Key Features of a Publishable Article</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Engaging title:</strong> Captures attention and hints at the content</li>\n  <li><strong>Strong opening (hook):</strong> A compelling fact, question, anecdote, or statement that draws readers in</li>\n  <li><strong>Clear structure:</strong> Logically organised paragraphs, each developing one aspect of the topic</li>\n  <li><strong>Appropriate tone:</strong> Matches the topic and target readership (informative, persuasive, reflective)</li>\n  <li><strong>Strong conclusion:</strong> Summarises key points or offers a final thought/call to action</li>\n  <li><strong>No salutation/address</strong> (unlike letters) — written for a general audience</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Techniques for an Engaging Opening (Hook)</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Startling statistic/fact</td><td>\'Over 70% of Nigerian youths are unemployed.\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rhetorical question</td><td>\'Have you ever wondered why our roads remain in such poor condition?\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Anecdote (brief story)</td><td>\'Last month, a young graduate in Lagos walked fifteen kilometres in search of work...\'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Direct statement of the issue</td><td>\'Youth unemployment has become one of Nigeria\'s most pressing challenges.\'</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Structuring the Body of an Article</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Each paragraph should focus on ONE main point/aspect of the topic</li>\n  <li>Use TRANSITION words to maintain flow between paragraphs (furthermore, however, in addition, as a result)</li>\n  <li>Support points with EVIDENCE, EXAMPLES, or ILLUSTRATIONS where appropriate</li>\n  <li>Maintain a CONSISTENT tone and viewpoint throughout</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Article Writing Process</h3>\n<ol class="learn-list">\n  <li>Choose an engaging, relevant title</li>\n  <li>Draft a compelling opening hook</li>\n  <li>Organise body paragraphs logically (one idea per paragraph)</li>\n  <li>Write a conclusion that reinforces the central message</li>\n  <li>Review for clarity, coherence, and appropriate tone</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Using the Dictionary (Oral/Spelling Component)</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Dictionaries provide: SPELLING, PRONUNCIATION (often in IPA), MEANING, WORD CLASS, ETYMOLOGY (word origin), and SAMPLE USAGE</li>\n  <li>Words are listed ALPHABETICALLY for quick reference</li>\n  <li>Guide words at the top of each page show the first and last entries on that page</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>arboreal</strong> (relating to or living in trees), <strong>camaraderie</strong> (mutual trust/friendship among a group), <strong>exalt</strong> (to praise/elevate highly), <strong>gaudy</strong> (extravagantly bright/showy, often in poor taste), <strong>mawkish</strong> (excessively sentimental), <strong>diffident</strong> (shy/lacking self-confidence), <strong>ornate</strong> (elaborately decorated).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Articles need NO salutation/address (unlike letters) — they begin directly with a TITLE and then a HOOK. Use varied opening techniques: statistics, rhetorical questions, anecdotes. Each body paragraph = ONE main point. WAEC marks articles on: appropriate format (title, no address), content development, language/register, and organisation.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Article features: engaging title, strong hook opening (statistic/question/anecdote), clear paragraph structure (one point each), appropriate tone, strong conclusion, NO salutation/address. Writing process: title → hook → organised body → conclusion → review. Dictionary use: spelling, pronunciation (IPA), meaning, word class, etymology, alphabetical organisation with guide words.\n</div>\n  ',
