    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Concord</span> (also called subject-verb agreement) is the grammatical rule that a verb must agree with its subject in NUMBER (singular/plural) and PERSON. Errors in concord are among the most frequently tested in WAEC Lexis and Structure.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Basic Rules of Concord</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Singular subject → singular verb: 'She WORKS hard.'</li>\n  <li>Plural subject → plural verb: 'They WORK hard.'</li>\n  <li>Compound subject joined by 'and' → plural verb: 'The teacher and the student ARE present.'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Tricky Concord Rules</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Collective nouns usually take singular verb (as a unit)</td><td>'The committee HAS made a decision.' (but 'The committee are divided in opinion' if acting individually)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>'Each', 'every', 'either', 'neither' → singular verb</td><td>'Each of the students HAS submitted.' / 'Neither answer IS correct.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Subjects joined by 'or/nor' → agree with the NEARER subject</td><td>'Either the teacher or the students ARE wrong.' / 'Either the students or the teacher IS wrong.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uncountable nouns → singular verb</td><td>'The information IS available.' / 'Furniture IS expensive.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Intervening phrases don't affect agreement</td><td>'The quality of these products IS poor.' (subject = quality, not products)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>'There is/are' → agree with the noun AFTER the verb</td><td>'There IS a problem.' / 'There ARE several problems.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Expressions of time, money, distance, weight (as a single unit) → singular</td><td>'Ten kilometres IS a long distance.' / 'Five thousand naira IS enough.'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Titles and names → singular</td><td>'Bleak House IS a novel by Dickens.'</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Common Concord Pitfalls</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>'News' is singular: 'The news IS good.'</li>\n  <li>'Mathematics', 'Physics', 'Economics' (academic subjects ending in -s) → singular: 'Mathematics IS difficult.'</li>\n  <li>Relative pronoun subjects agree with the antecedent: 'It is I who AM responsible.' (not 'I who is')</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Selection)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>labyrinthine</strong> (like a labyrinth; complex/intricate), <strong>garrulous</strong> (excessively talkative), <strong>ludicrous</strong> (absurd, ridiculous), <strong>ostentatious</strong> (displaying wealth conspicuously), <strong>propitious</strong> (giving a good chance of success; favourable), <strong>taciturn</strong> (habitually silent, saying little), <strong>loquacious</strong> (tending to talk a great deal).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Ignore phrases BETWEEN subject and verb (e.g., 'The quality OF THESE PRODUCTS is...' — the verb agrees with 'quality', not 'products'). Or/nor rule: verb agrees with the NEARER subject. 'News', 'mathematics', 'physics', 'economics', 'politics' take SINGULAR verbs. 'Each', 'every', 'either', 'neither' = SINGULAR. Collective nouns (committee, team, jury) usually take SINGULAR verbs.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Concord = verb must agree with subject in number. Tricky rules: collective nouns (singular as unit), each/every/either/neither (singular), or/nor (agree with nearer subject), uncountable nouns (singular), phrases between subject and verb (don't affect agreement), there is/are (agree with noun after verb), expressions of quantity as single units (singular). News/maths/physics/economics = singular.\n</div>\n  ",
