    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  An <span class="learn-keyword">idiom</span> is a phrase or expression whose meaning cannot be deduced from its individual words — the meaning is figurative, not literal. Idioms are culturally embedded expressions frequently tested in WAEC Vocabulary and Usage questions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Common English Idioms and Their Meanings</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Idiom</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Hit the nail on the head</td><td>To say exactly the right thing; to be exactly correct</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Beat around the bush</td><td>To avoid coming directly to the point</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bite the bullet</td><td>To endure a painful or difficult situation with courage</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Burn the midnight oil</td><td>To work or study very late into the night</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cost an arm and a leg</td><td>To be very expensive</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Let the cat out of the bag</td><td>To accidentally reveal a secret</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Turn a blind eye</td><td>To choose to ignore something one knows is wrong</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bite off more than one can chew</td><td>To attempt more than one can handle</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Under the weather</td><td>Feeling unwell, slightly ill</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Spill the beans</td><td>To reveal secret information</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Miss the boat</td><td>To miss an opportunity</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sit on the fence</td><td>To be neutral; to avoid taking sides in a dispute</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bite the hand that feeds you</td><td>To harm or act against those who are helping you</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Break a leg</td><td>Good luck (used especially in theatre)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Kick the bucket</td><td>To die (informal/euphemistic)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Critical Reading Techniques</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Identifying the main idea:</strong> Look for the topic sentence (usually first or last sentence of a paragraph)</li>\n  <li><strong>Inference:</strong> Drawing conclusions beyond what is explicitly stated, based on clues in the text</li>\n  <li><strong>Distinguishing fact from opinion:</strong> Facts can be verified; opinions express views or judgments</li>\n  <li><strong>Identifying the writer\'s purpose:</strong> To inform, persuade, entertain, describe, or argue</li>\n  <li><strong>Vocabulary in context:</strong> Using surrounding context clues to determine a word\'s meaning</li>\n  <li><strong>Tone/attitude:</strong> The writer\'s feeling toward the subject (critical, sympathetic, ironic, objective)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Vocabulary: Words of the Week (Final Selection)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>acrimony</strong> (bitterness and ill feeling), <strong>prolific</strong> (producing many works/much output), <strong>ebullient</strong> (cheerful and full of energy), <strong>idiosyncrasy</strong> (a distinctive habit or mannerism), <strong>ambiguous</strong> (open to more than one interpretation), <strong>cursory</strong> (hasty and not thorough), <strong>didactic</strong> (intended to teach a moral lesson).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC tests idioms in CONTEXT — the phrase is underlined in a sentence, and you must choose its meaning from four options. Key: idiom meanings are NEVER literal (if someone \'lets the cat out of the bag\', no actual cat or bag is involved). Critical reading: INFER (conclude from context), DISTINGUISH (fact vs opinion), IDENTIFY (main idea/purpose/tone). Vocabulary-in-context: use surrounding sentences to determine meaning.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Idioms have FIGURATIVE meanings that cannot be deduced literally. Key idioms: hit the nail on the head (exactly right), beat around the bush (avoid the point), burn midnight oil (work late), let the cat out of the bag (reveal secret), turn a blind eye (ignore deliberately), sit on the fence (be neutral), cost an arm and a leg (very expensive). Critical reading: main idea, inference, fact vs opinion, purpose, tone, vocabulary in context.\n</div>\n  ',
