    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  This final topic provides a comprehensive review of the key concepts covered across all three terms of SS2 English Language, organised for effective exam preparation. Each major skill area is synthesised with the most important rules and patterns.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Summary of Key Grammar Rules</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Key Rule</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Nouns</td><td>Countable vs uncountable; collective noun agreement; plural forms (irregular, zero plural, foreign)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pronouns</td><td>Subject/object case (I/me); possessive determiner vs pronoun (my/mine); who/whom; reflexive forms</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Verb tenses</td><td>Sequence of tenses: backshift in reported speech; conditionals (zero/first/second/third)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Active/Passive</td><td>Formula: BE + past participle; intransitive verbs cannot be passivised</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Concord</td><td>Each/every/neither = singular; or/nor = agree with nearer subject; uncountable = singular; news/maths = singular</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Adjuncts/Conjuncts/Disjuncts</td><td>Adjunct: modifies verb; Conjunct: links clauses; Disjunct: comments on whole sentence</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Summary of Writing Genres and Their Distinctions</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Genre</th><th>Key Distinguishing Feature</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Formal letter</td><td>Address+date, salutation (Dear Sir/Madam or Dear Mr. [Name]), close (Yours faithfully/sincerely), full name</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Informal letter</td><td>First name salutation (Dear [Name]), contractions allowed, Love/Best wishes close, first name signature</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Article</td><td>Catchy title, no address/salutation, general readership, engaging hook</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Speech</td><td>Direct audience address (Ladies and gentlemen...), rhetorical devices, spoken delivery style</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Expository essay</td><td>Informs/explains multiple aspects; balanced, no clear \'for/against\' position required</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Argumentative essay</td><td>Takes a CLEAR POSITION, persuades, addresses counter-arguments</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Summary</td><td>Own words, main ideas only, within word limit, no personal opinion added</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Oral English Review</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Voiced/voiceless pairs: p/b, t/d, k/g, f/v, s/z, θ/ð, ʃ/ʒ, tʃ/dʒ</li>\n  <li>Always voiced: m, n, ŋ, l, r, w, j</li>\n  <li>Word stress: nouns stress first syllable; verbs often second; -tion/-sion suffix stresses syllable before</li>\n  <li>Emphatic stress shifts focus and meaning of a sentence</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Vocabulary Review: Selected Final Words</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>salubrious</strong> (health-giving; pleasant and clean), <strong>intrepid</strong> (fearless and adventurous), <strong>recalcitrant</strong> (stubbornly resistant to authority), <strong>perspicacious</strong> (having a ready insight; shrewd), <strong>indefatigable</strong> (persisting tirelessly), <strong>sycophant</strong> (a person who flatters powerful people for personal gain), <strong>tenacious</strong> (holding firm to something; determined).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Final Exam Tips:</strong> (1) Genre identification: note what the question asks for BEFORE writing. (2) Avoid concord errors: always identify the TRUE subject (ignore intervening phrases). (3) Reported speech: always backshift if reporting verb is PAST (unless universal truth). (4) Idioms: the FIGURATIVE meaning is always the answer. (5) Summary: ALWAYS use your own words and NEVER add personal opinion. (6) Letter vs Article: letter needs addresses/salutation; article needs title only.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Synthesis:</strong> All writing genres share core requirements: clear purpose, logical organisation, appropriate register/tone, and strong conclusion. Grammar across all topics shares the same underlying logic: identify the true subject/agent, match verbs correctly, maintain appropriate tense sequence, and use appropriate lexical choices for context and register.\n</div>\n  ',
